\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Symmetric and alternating forms on the real quaternion fiber}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{For the left #{2T} action on #{\mathbb H}, every invariant
real bilinear form is uniquely
##{c_a(x,y)=\langle x,ya\rangle_0,\qquad a\in\mathbb H.}
It is symmetric exactly when #{a} is real, alternating
exactly when #{\operatorname{Re}(a)=0}, and perfect exactly
when #{a\ne 0}. Positive real #{a} gives a positive symmetric
form. The representation is irreducible of quaternionic
commutant type.}

\proof{
\p{The units #{1,\qi,\qj,h} span #{\mathbb H} over
#{\mathbb R}. The unit-span proof in \ref{fdip-00V5}
therefore identifies the commutant with right multiplication,
with its opposite product. An invariant real subspace is
stable under every left quaternion multiplication. If it
contains #{x\ne 0}, then for every #{y}, left multiplication
by #{yx^{-1}} sends #{x} to #{y}. Thus it is all of
#{\mathbb H}, proving irreducibility. Quaternion conjugation
identifies #{\mathbb H^{\mathrm{op}}} with #{\mathbb H}.}

\p{The positive norm pairing is perfect and invariant.
As in \ref{fdip-00V5}, it identifies invariant bilinear
forms with commuting endomorphisms, proving the unique
parametrization. The norm adjoint of #{R_a} is #{R_{\bar a}},
by the scalar-part calculation in \ref{fdip-00V4}.
Consequently symmetry is equivalent to #{a=\bar a}, which
means #{a\in\mathbb R}. Also
##{c_a(x,x)=\operatorname{Re}(\bar xxa)
 =N(x)\operatorname{Re}(a).}
Alternation is therefore equivalent to
#{\operatorname{Re}(a)=0}, with necessity tested at #{x=1}.
Perfectness is equivalent to invertibility of #{R_a}; every
nonzero quaternion has inverse #{\bar a/N(a)}.
For positive real #{a}, the displayed diagonal is positive
at every nonzero #{x}, proving the positive symmetric case.}
}

\p{The quaternionic commutant thus supports both positive
symmetric and nonzero alternating forms. A commutant label
alone does not select one of them.}
